  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $P(x)<0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{3}(x-1)(x+2)(x-2)$ are $x=0,$ $x=1$ and $x=\\pm 2,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $3,$ and\n\n- $x=1$ and $x=\\pm 2$ are simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$ with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033945/assets/a37f1c62868581f74a60c073adabf59e2fc66cb17135ffa48df1c76eb22803c8.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)<0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is below the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (1,2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
